In this conversation, James prompted discussions on modeling sea-level rise (SLR) impacts in his model, referencing Yumashev et al. (2021). The focus was on the introduction of a Gamma distribution for the time constant \(\tau_{\text{SLR}}\) in SLR dynamics, which helps avoid double-counting discontinuity-type impacts. Key equations and calibration techniques were highlighted, leading to a Monte Carlo mean SLR estimate of 1.57 meters by 2100 under RCP8.5. Additionally, the Coastal Impact and Adaptation Model (CIAM) was discussed as a tool to evaluate SLR impacts on coastlines, incorporating adaptation strategies. CIAM's structure, including the optimization problem for social planners and categorization of coastal impacts, was explained, with references to relevant literature. The conversation aimed to succinctly summarize details for the Supplementary Information of a follow-up paper.
